<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marienstatt Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marienstatt Abbey is a Cistercian monastery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a> near Hachenburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>. Founded in the early 13th century under the patronage of the counts of Sayn, it has an early Gothic church, one of the first Gothic buildings east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Dissolved in 1803, it was resettled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a> in 1888 and is still active.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marienstatt-abbey.jpg" alt="Marienstatt Abbey" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolkenkratzer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abtei_Marienstatt_008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The abbey stood in a direct line of Cistercian foundations: it was settled from Heisterbach Abbey, which had been founded from Himmerod Abbey, itself a daughter of Clairvaux Abbey in 1134. The community was first established in 1212 at Neunkhausen, made possible by a donation from Eberhard of Arenberg and his wife Adelheid von Molsberg. Climate and soil proved poor there, and in 1222 the monks moved to the present site in the Nister valley. According to legend, the abbot chose it after dreaming of a hawthorn bush that flowered in winter. The first church was consecrated on 27 December 1227 under Conrad, the third abbot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1476 lay people could attend services in the abbey church, and in 1485 the octave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> became its pilgrimage day. The Counts of Sayn introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in their territories from 1561. In the Thirty Years' War Sweden claimed the property as crown land on 3 October 1633, expelling the monks and damaging the buildings; in 1637 abbot Johannes Wittig lived there with only one brother and one novice. After the war the monastery recovered, the church was decorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style under abbot Benedikt Bach, and the old buildings were replaced from 1735 to 1751 under Petrus Emons.</p><p>During secularisation the abbey was given to Frederick William, Prince of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a>, and the last monks left in 1803, though Catholic masses continued; from 1831 the church served a new parish, which saved it from demolition. In 1864 Bishop Peter Joseph Blum of Limburg bought the site and opened a home for neglected boys. In 1888 monks of Wettingen-Mehrerau Abbey bought it back and resettled it under Dominikus Willi, later Bishop of Limburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Abbot Konrad II Kolb built a library in 1909, and the school he opened in 1910 grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> into the Privates Gymnasium Marienstatt; in 2024 the historian Uli Jungbluth published former pupils' accounts of harsh treatment at its boarding school between 1947 and 1954. The church belongs to the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a> and the other buildings to the abbey, which keeps a brewery, a guest house and a library of about 112,700 books. Its organ, built by Franz Rieger in 1969-1970 and later enlarged, has 67 stops.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marienstatt Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts ruled hilly lands with iron mines and abbeys such as Sayn Abbey and <b>Marienstatt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sayn">County of Sayn</a></li></ul></section>
